{
  "image_name": "746dc40e368a696135277a27e4492fbb.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several machine-learning representations and model classes used for crystalline materials. Overall, it shows a pipeline from encoding atomic structure to predicting properties, generating new candidates, and extracting simple descriptors.\n\nOne theme is how a crystal can be turned into a graph: atoms become nodes, interatomic distances define connections, and local geometric information is converted into feature vectors. These graph-based inputs are then processed by graph convolutional networks, where repeated convolution, hidden layers, and pooling combine local atomic environments into a final output prediction.\n\nAnother theme is alternative structure representations. Property-labelled fragments are built from local environments, apparently using Voronoi-based tessellation, and linked into a global periodic graph. Variational autoencoders map known materials into a latent space that is organized by property and synthesizability, then decode points in that space into proposed new, potentially synthesizable materials.\n\nThe bottom row highlights symbolic methods. Genetic programming evolves analytical formulas by crossover and mutation, producing compact expressions from simpler ones. SISSO searches an enormous feature space by selecting promising subspaces and constructing low-dimensional descriptors. Together, the figure compares complementary strategies for learning structure–property relationships and discovering materials from crystal data.",
